id: pm-rl-mpd9
title: "Metric series live in item history, never in an item body"
description: "A run emits a monotonically growing series of metric events. Written to an item body, which is a scalar field, two concurrent writers produce a genuine conflict on every write, and a conflicted body is unparseable. Written to the append-only history stream, both sides' events survive a merge as their union, which pm-vcs proved empirically against a real git merge rather than by assumption. This single rule is what makes concurrent sweeps merge cleanly, so it is a constraint on every command that records a metric, not a convention."
type: Decision
status: closed
priority: 2
tags: []
created_at: "2026-07-31T06:38:29.315Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-13T21:12:44.481Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-01T12:07:57.135Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-01T12:07:57.135Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
acceptance_criteria: No command writes a metric value to an item body or any scalar field; a test merges two branches that both logged to one run and asserts the union of events with zero conflicts and zero loss
release: 2026.7.31
resolution: Every metric uses one canonical append-only pm note; no metric command changes the item body or another scalar series field.
expected_result: Concurrent metrics survive branch merges as a complete union with no scalar conflict.
actual_result: A real Git merge preserves both branch-specific metric events and the command reads them in step order.
